from pathlib import Path
Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "The error handling strategy in `_AppCtxGlobals` (defined in `src/flask/ctx.py`) balances strict exception raising with optional default values through different method behaviors:\\n\\n**Strict Error Raising (`__getattr__` and `__delattr__`):**\\n\\nBoth `__getattr__` (lines 52-56) and `__delattr__` (lines 61-65) always raise `AttributeError` for missing attributes, with no default value option:\\n\\n```python\\ndef __getattr__(self, name: str) -> t.Any:\\n    try:\\n        return self.__dict__[name]\\n    except KeyError:\\n        raise AttributeError(name) from None\\n\\ndef __delattr__(self, name: str) -> None:\\n    try:\\n        del self.__dict__[name]\\n    except KeyError:\\n        raise AttributeError(name) from None\\n```\\n\\nThis enforces explicit attribute existence when using direct attribute access (`g.attr`) or deletion (`del g.attr`).\\n\\n**Flexible Default Provision (`pop` method):**\\n\\nThe `pop` method (lines 78-90) uses a sentinel pattern to provide optional defaults:\\n\\n```python\\ndef pop(self, name: str, default: t.Any = _sentinel) -> t.Any:\\n    if default is _sentinel:\\n        return self.__dict__.pop(name)  # Raises KeyError if missing\\n    else:\\n        return self.__dict__.pop(name, default)  # Returns default if missing\\n```\\n\\nWhen called without a default (`g.pop(\'attr\')`), it raises `KeyError` for missing attributes. When called with a default (`g.pop(\'attr\', None)`), it returns the default instead of raising an exception.\\n\\n**Additional Defensive Methods:**\\n\\nThe class also provides a `get` method (lines 67-76) that always accepts a default value, and `__contains__` (line 104) for membership testing, enabling defensive patterns like:\\n\\n```python\\nif \'db\' not in g:\\n    g.db = connect_to_database()\\ndb = g.pop(\'db\', None)  # Safe removal with default\\n```\\n\\nThis design allows developers to choose between strict error detection (via `__getattr__`/`__delattr__`) and defensive programming (via `pop` with defaults, `get`, or `__contains__` checks), as demonstrated in Flask\'s documentation (`docs/appcontext.rst` lines 114-124) and tutorial examples."}' + '\n')
